Finding: {"title":"Two functions named stripCodeFences with different, non-equivalent behavior — the weaker one guards the highest-stakes AI parse","severity":"MEDIUM","area":"AI response parsing / code duplication","files":["src/server/routes/routes-ai-curriculum-builder.ts","src/lib/import/helpers.ts","src/lib/inngest/functions/curriculumBuilder.ts"],"detail":"`routes-ai-curriculum-builder.ts:578-583` defines `stripCodeFences` using anchored regex (`^```...`, `...```$`) that only strips fences at the exact start/end of the string. `src/lib/import/helpers.ts:413-420` defines a different `stripCodeFences` with an explicit comment: 'Strip markdown code fences from AI response, including when preamble text precedes the fence' — using a non-anchored match specifically to handle cases like 'Here is the JSON: ```json {...} ```'. `routes-ai-curriculum-incremental.ts` imports and uses the robust helpers.ts version; but `curriculumBuilder.ts` (the Inngest job doing the single largest, most expensive 32000-token curriculum generation) imports and uses the weaker builder.ts version.","impact":"If Claude prepends any conversational preamble before the JSON code fence in the full one-shot curriculum build (more likely on the largest/most complex generations, which is exactly this endpoint), the weaker `stripCodeFences` leaves the preamble text in the string, `safeParseAIJson` fails to parse it, and the build fails — a failure mode the codebase already solved for the import path but did not carry over to the builder path.","recommendation":"Delete the weaker duplicate in routes-ai-curriculum-builder.ts and have all curriculum-builder consumers import the robust version from lib/import/helpers.ts (already done correctly in routes-ai-curriculum-incremental.ts).","confidence":"high"}
